From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 19:15:35 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] context: recover and inspect leaked VM stack state

Two paths leave state on the VM that nothing pops, and both end the same
way: the residue accumulates until stack_size_limit trips, and from then
on every execution fails at once with "exceeded maximum call stack
length". The embedder is left with a context that can no longer run JS.

- Error unwinds that cross host re-entry boundaries can leak vm.stack
  entries, for example when a host trampoline re-enters a suspended
  context through a raw pointer. One observed case: React
  hydration-mismatch throws inside animation rAF callbacks leaked a few
  slots per tick, and the context died after about 45 seconds.
- A panic out of the interpreter (boa's own `index out of bounds` on a
  minified third-party script, say) unwinds straight past every
  `pop_frame`, so the frames it was running stay on `vm.frames` and
  their registers stay on `vm.stack` for the life of the context.

ks_clear_residual_stack recovers both at an idle point. With no frame
other than the VM's permanent dummy frame at index 0 (frames.len() <=
1), the value stack must be empty too, so it is drained. Stranded frames
are reclaimed too, gated on `ks_execution_depth() == 0`: with no
bytecode run live on the native stack, whatever is on the VM is by
definition unreachable. The counter is a thread-local kept by a Drop
guard in `run`/`run_async_with_budget`, so it unwinds with the panic
rather than counting it forever. Popping (not truncating) restores the
environment and realm each frame saved, the same way a normal return
does. The VM's dummy frame is never popped.

JsObject::call/construct track host_call_depth with a manual increment
and decrement that a panic also skips, which inflates the recursion
count for good, so the reclaim resets it too.

ks_vm_stats is a diagnostic companion. The idle-point drain never fired
on the poisoned-context repro because frames were non-empty, so the
stack growth is pinned by live suspended executions, not residue. Expose
(frames, stack) so the embedder can log occupancy over time and find
what accumulates. Both probes skip the VM's permanent dummy frame, so
frames == 0 means idle.
---
 src/context/mod.rs | 36 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 src/vm/mod.rs      | 43 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 79 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/context/mod.rs b/src/context/mod.rs
index 78453d2..982dedf 100644
--- a/src/context/mod.rs
+++ b/src/context/mod.rs
@@ -494,6 +494,42 @@ impl Context {
         self.job_executor().enqueue_job(job, self);
     }

+    /// With no bytecode run live, whatever an unwind or panic left on the VM
+    /// is garbage that would trip the limits forever; reclaim it, returning the slots freed.
+    #[inline]
+    pub fn ks_clear_residual_stack(&mut self) -> usize {
+        // A panic unwind skips every `pop_frame` and every host-call decrement, so
+        // stranded frames pin their registers and the recursion count never drops.
+        if crate::vm::ks_execution_depth() != 0 {
+            return 0;
+        }
+        while self.vm.pop_frame().is_some() {}
+        self.vm.host_call_depth = 0;
+        self.vm.pending_exception = None;
+        self.vm.set_return_value(JsValue::undefined());
+        let n = self.vm.stack.len();
+        self.vm.stack.clear();
+        n
+    }
+
+    /// (call frames excluding the dummy, value-stack slots). frames == 0
+    /// with slots left is leaked residue; frames > 0 is a suspended execution.
+    #[inline]
+    pub fn ks_vm_stats(&self) -> (usize, usize) {
+        (self.vm.frames.len().saturating_sub(1), self.vm.stack.len())
+    }
+
+    /// Names of the frames currently pinning the VM, bottom-up (the
+    /// dummy frame excluded). Identifies which suspended execution never completes.
+    pub fn ks_vm_frame_names(&self) -> Vec<String> {
+        self.vm
+            .frames
+            .iter()
+            .skip(1)
+            .map(|f| f.code_block().name().to_std_string_escaped())
+            .collect()
+    }
+
     /// Runs all the jobs with the provided job executor.
     #[inline]
     pub fn run_jobs(&mut self) -> JsResult<()> {
diff --git a/src/vm/mod.rs b/src/vm/mod.rs
index 5f6170b..85f3b47 100644
--- a/src/vm/mod.rs
+++ b/src/vm/mod.rs
@@ -133,6 +133,18 @@ impl Stack {
         }
     }

+    /// Number of live value slots, for the occupancy and residual-stack
+    /// probes on Context (ks_vm_stats, ks_clear_residual_stack).
+    pub(crate) fn len(&self) -> usize {
+        self.stack.len()
+    }
+
+    /// Drop every value slot. Only sound when no frame is executing,
+    /// which ks_clear_residual_stack checks first.
+    pub(crate) fn clear(&mut self) {
+        self.stack.clear();
+    }
+
     /// Get a register value by index, relative to the given frame's `rp`.
     pub(crate) fn get_register(&self, frame: &CallFrame, index: usize) -> Option<&JsValue> {
         self.stack.get(frame.rp as usize + index)
@@ -675,6 +687,35 @@ impl Vm {
     }
 }

+// How many bytecode runs are live on the native stack. An embedder that
+// survives panics needs to know when what is left on the VM is garbage.
+thread_local! {
+    static KS_EXEC_DEPTH: std::cell::Cell<usize> = const { std::cell::Cell::new(0) };
+}
+
+/// Zero means no `run`/`run_async_with_budget` is executing, so anything
+/// left on `vm.frames`/`vm.stack` was stranded by a panic unwind.
+pub(crate) fn ks_execution_depth() -> usize {
+    KS_EXEC_DEPTH.with(std::cell::Cell::get)
+}
+
+// Drop (not a plain decrement) so the count is restored while a panic unwinds
+// through the interpreter, which is the case that matters.
+struct KsExecDepthGuard;
+
+impl KsExecDepthGuard {
+    fn enter() -> Self {
+        KS_EXEC_DEPTH.with(|d| d.set(d.get() + 1));
+        Self
+    }
+}
+
+impl Drop for KsExecDepthGuard {
+    fn drop(&mut self) {
+        KS_EXEC_DEPTH.with(|d| d.set(d.get().saturating_sub(1)));
+    }
+}
+
